==== NAME
mkfs.pdxfs — format a file or block-device-capability target with a
fresh PDXB v1 superblock, allocator bitmap, journal ring, and root
inode, for paideia-os volume tooling.

==== SYNOPSIS
    mkfs.pdxfs [--force] [--label=<str>] [--journal-size=<n_blocks>]
               [--dry-run] [--verbose] [--sig-key=<key-cap-uri>]
               [--upgrade] <target>

`<target>` is either a filesystem path (dev workflow, no root, no
signing required — begins with `/`, `~`, or `.`) or a `cap:blkdev:`
device-capability URI (production workflow, elevate-required, signing
mandatory).

==== DESCRIPTION
`mkfs.pdxfs` is a standalone ring-3 ELF — its own `_start`, not a
shared library — that writes a fresh PDXB v1 superblock, a zeroed
allocator bitmap, a zeroed write-ahead-log journal ring, and a single
real root-directory inode (slot 1) onto `<target>`. It is the first of
three actions that bring a blank disk or image file online for PdxFS
v1: **format** (this tool), then **mount** (`mount.pdxfs`), with
`umount.pdxfs` as the reversal. See [[cref: OPTIONS]] for the full flag
reference, [[cref: EXIT-CODES]] for the exit-status contract, and
[[cref: RECORD]] for the `PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1` shape every invocation
emits on stdout.

`mkfs.pdxfs` performs real, direct syscalls against `<target>`'s bare
path when it is a file (no capability is minted or consumed for this —
see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]]); it links `libpdx-volume` (real superblock
codec + ML-DSA-65 signing) and `libpdx-audit` (real per-invocation
journal-record open/commit, though the daemon dispatch behind it is
still stubbed).

==== OPTIONS

---- --force
Bypass the non-blank-target refusal gate ([[cref: REFUSAL]])
unconditionally, regardless of what `<target>`'s leading bytes look
like. Does NOT bypass the audit trail — every invocation, forced or
not, opens and commits an audit record (`AuditWire::mkfs_audit_begin`/
`mkfs_audit_commit`), distinguished by `op_name`
(`"mkfs.pdxfs.format"` vs `"mkfs.pdxfs.format.force"`).

---- --label=<str>
An operator-facing label string, printed verbatim in the `--dry-run`
preview record. Not yet written into the on-disk superblock (no
`label` field is stamped by `mkfs_format_build_superblock` at this
landing).

---- --journal-size=<n_blocks>
Journal-ring size in 4096-byte BLOCKS (not bytes — see
[[cref: LIMITATIONS]] for a task-brief-vs-implementation reconciliation
note). Defaults to 1024 blocks (4 MiB) when not given.

---- --dry-run
Print the `PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1` preview line ([[cref: RECORD]]) and
exit 0 without performing any write of any kind. File targets only —
see [[cref: EXIT-CODES]] for what a `--dry-run` device-cap or invalid
target does instead.

---- --verbose
Parsed and stored (`ParsedArgv.flags & PA_FLAG_VERBOSE`); not yet
consulted by any code path in this repo.

---- --sig-key=<key-cap-uri>
Parsed and stored as a raw pointer to the cap-URI string; NEVER
dereferenced or resolved into real key material by any code path in
this repo. Every superblock this tool signs uses a PLACEHOLDER
all-zero 32-byte seed regardless of this flag's value — see
[[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

---- --upgrade
**STUB, unwired.** Parses into `ParsedArgv.flags & PA_FLAG_UPGRADE`;
no dispatch branch in this tool's `_start` currently reads that bit. A
future PDXL->PDXB rewrite body (`Upgrade::mkfs_upgrade_run`) exists and
is directly callable but is not reachable from any argv-driven
invocation of the compiled tool at this landing. See
[[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

==== EXIT-CODES
Per `design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md`'s own exit-code table,
extended at M3 for device-target and elevation outcomes:

    0   OK — the write (or --dry-run preview) succeeded.
    1   argv missing <target>, OR "not yet implemented" (a
        TARGET_INVALID target — any prefix other than `/`, `~`, `.`,
        or `cap:blkdev:`).
    2   argv parse error (missing target — same code as 1's first case;
        distinguished only by the stderr diagnostic text).
    3   MKFS_EXIT_SYSCALL_ERROR — an open/write/encode/sign failure
        during the real write pipeline.
    4   MKFS_EXIT_DEVICE_STUB — a `cap:blkdev:` target reached the
        (documented-stub) write-attempt arm. UNREACHABLE via normal
        invocation at this landing, since elevation always denies
        first (exit 6) — see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].
    5   MKFS_EXIT_REFUSED — the non-blank-target refusal gate fired
        and `--force` was not given.
    6   MKFS_EXIT_ELEVATION_DENIED — a `cap:blkdev:` target's
        elevation check denied (the ONLY outcome this landing's
        elevation stub can produce — every `cap:blkdev:` invocation
        exits 6).
    7   MKFS_EXIT_UPGRADE_NOT_IMPLEMENTED — `Upgrade::mkfs_upgrade_run`'s
        own stub return code. Not reachable via any argv-driven
        invocation yet (see `--upgrade` above).

==== RECORD
Every invocation emits exactly one `PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1` line to
stdout, preceded by one documented deferral header line (`# semantic-
pipe emit deferred (svc.schema-registry not yet live, paideia-os#2000)
-- PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 line-based fallback below`) — this tool does
not yet bind a real `libpdx-semantic-pipe` schema (see
[[cref: LIMITATIONS]]). Two shapes:

    PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { target: <path>, label: <label>,
                            journal_size: <N>, dry_run: true }

emitted only by `--dry-run` against a file target, and

    PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { target: <path>, result_code: <CODE> }
    PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { target: <path>, result_code: FAIL, error: <errno> }

emitted by every real-write, refusal, device-stub, elevation-deny, or
`--upgrade`-stub outcome, where `<CODE>` is one of `SIGNED_OK`,
`REFUSED_ALREADY_PDXB`, `REFUSED_ALREADY_PDXL`, `REFUSED_NON_BLANK`,
`DEVICE_TARGET_STUB`, `ELEVATION_DENIED`, `AUDIT_FAIL` (defined,
unreachable at this landing), or `UPGRADE_NOT_IMPLEMENTED` (reachable
only via a direct call to `Upgrade::mkfs_upgrade_run`, not via argv —
see `--upgrade` above). `FAIL` additionally carries a positive `errno`
field. This is a REAL rendering of the caller's actual arguments and
outcome in every case — none of these fields is a fixed placeholder
value.

==== EXAMPLES
    mkfs.pdxfs --dry-run --label=data --journal-size=2048 /tmp/foo.img

prints the four-field preview line and exits 0 without writing
anything.

    mkfs.pdxfs /tmp/foo.img

really formats `/tmp/foo.img`: a signed PDXB v1 superblock, a zeroed
inode table with a real root-directory inode at slot 1, a zeroed
allocator bitmap, and a zeroed journal ring (1024 blocks by default).
Prints `PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { target: /tmp/foo.img, result_code:
SIGNED_OK }` and exits 0.

    mkfs.pdxfs /tmp/foo.img

run a second time against the same now-formatted target: refuses
(`result_code: REFUSED_ALREADY_PDXB`, exit 5) without `--force`.

    mkfs.pdxfs --force /tmp/foo.img

bypasses the refusal and rewrites the target (`result_code:
SIGNED_OK`, exit 0).

    mkfs.pdxfs cap:blkdev:0x05

exits 6 (`ELEVATION_DENIED`) — device-cap targets always fail closed
at this landing (no broker-endpoint capability this tool could use to
prove real elevation).

==== LIMITATIONS
Cited from `design/architecture.md`; each item names the section with
the full rationale.

- **Device-target writes are fully stubbed.** No `sys_cap_query`-
  equivalent primitive exists to validate a `cap:blkdev:` slot's kind,
  and no block-granularity write syscall exists in this kernel at any
  sysno (§11.1). A `cap:blkdev:` target always fails elevation first
  (exit 6) — the write-stub arm (exit 4) is real, wired code but
  unreachable via normal invocation.
- **Superblock signatures use a placeholder all-zero seed.**
  `--sig-key=` is parsed and stored but never resolved into real key
  material — every signature is well-formed (a real ML-DSA-65
  intrinsic call over the correct message) but verifies against no
  real key (§11.2).
- **`libpdx-semantic-pipe` is not linked.** Every record stays the
  line-based `sys_write` rendering described in [[cref: RECORD]] until
  `svc.schema-registry` lands (§11.3).
- **`--upgrade` is unwired.** The flag parses and a stub target
  function exists, but no dispatch branch calls it, and no PDXL
  on-disk decoder exists anywhere in `libpdx-volume` to rewrite from
  (`src/upgrade.pdx`'s own module header).
- **No `--size=`-equivalent flag.** Every volume this tool formats has
  a zero-sized data region (`data_lba`/`data_bcount` = 0) — there is
  no flag to size a real data region against yet (§9.1, §11.6).
- **`--journal-size` is in BLOCKS, not bytes**, matching this repo's
  own frozen argv default (1024 blocks = 4 MiB) — a task-brief
  reconciliation note, not an upstream design-doc discrepancy (§9.2).
- **The refusal gate inspects only the first 4 bytes** of an existing
  target, not the full first 4096-byte block the upstream design doc's
  fuller text describes, and does not hex-dump on refusal (§9.5).
- **No `KIND_PDXFS_FILE` capability is minted or consumed anywhere.**
  Every file-target syscall in this repo operates on the bare
  filesystem path string, not a cap-invoke (`caps.decl`'s own note,
  §10).

==== SEE-ALSO
Upstream design: `design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md` §2.1 + §3 +
§9.1 and `design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md` §2.1 + §2.3 in the
paideia-os repo. Repo-internal shape: `design/architecture.md` in this
repo. Per-driver test contracts: `tests/README.md`. Release workflow:
`release/RELEASE-1.0.0.md`. Companion tools: `mount.pdxfs`,
`umount.pdxfs` (not yet created as repos). Companion library:
`libpdx-volume` (the PDXB codec + signing this tool links).

==== VERSION
mkfs.pdxfs 1.0.0 (R53 wave, M1..M5 close). The actual link + dual-sign
+ mirror-push run happens once substrates S1 (paideia-as toolchain),
S2 (pkgs.paideia-os endpoint), S3 (a live release-line seed key), and
S5 (a real link pass against `libpdx-volume`/`libpdx-audit`) go green,
per `release/RELEASE-1.0.0.md` §3.
